Azure Functions Configuration

The project uses the Azure Functions isolated worker model.

The local Functions runtime uses:

FUNCTIONS_WORKER_RUNTIME=dotnet-isolated

Local Azure Storage is configured using Azurite.

The local storage setting uses:

AzureWebJobsStorage=UseDevelopmentStorage=true

The local settings file is excluded from source control because it contains local development configuration.

Azurite

Azurite is used as the local emulator for Azure Storage during development.

The Azurite Docker container can be started with:

docker run -d --name coffeenchill-azurite `
  -p 10000:10000 `
  -p 10001:10001 `
  -p 10002:10002 `
  mcr.microsoft.com/azure-storage/azurite

To check whether the container is running:

docker ps

If the container already exists but is stopped:

docker start coffeenchill-azurite
Azure Table Storage — Menu Items

Menu items are stored using Azure Table Storage.

The table used by the application is:

MenuItems

Each menu item uses:

PartitionKey = Category
RowKey = ID

The menu item contains:

Property	Description
Category	Menu category
ID	Unique menu item identifier
Name	Name of the menu item
Description	Description of the item
Price	Price of the item
IsAvailable	Whether the item is currently available

The Azure Table entity implements ITableEntity so that PartitionKey and RowKey can be used directly by Azure Table Storage.

Menu Create Endpoint
POST /api/menu

The Create Menu Item endpoint creates a new menu item in Azure Table Storage.

Request
POST http://localhost:7071/api/menu
Example JSON
{
  "category": "Hot Drinks",
  "id": "COF-100",
  "name": "Cappuccino",
  "description": "Espresso with steamed milk and foam",
  "price": 38.50,
  "isAvailable": true
}
Expected response

The endpoint returns:

201 Created

Example:

{
  "message": "Menu item created successfully.",
  "item": {
    "category": "Hot Drinks",
    "id": "COF-100",
    "name": "Cappuccino",
    "description": "Espresso with steamed milk and foam",
    "price": 38.5,
    "isAvailable": true
  }
}
Menu Read Endpoint
GET /api/menu

The Get All Menu Items endpoint retrieves all menu items stored in the MenuItems Azure Table.

Request
GET http://localhost:7071/api/menu
Expected response

The endpoint returns:

200 OK

Example:

[
  {
    "category": "Hot Drinks",
    "id": "COF-100",
    "name": "Cappuccino",
    "description": "Espresso with steamed milk and foam",
    "price": 38.5,
    "isAvailable": true
  }
]
Running the Azure Functions Locally

Azure Functions Core Tools v4 is installed on the development machine.

The installed version can be checked with:

func --version

Current installed version:

4.14.0

The Functions Core Tools executable is located at:

C:\Program Files\Microsoft\Azure Functions Core Tools\func.exe

The CoffeeNChill Functions project is located at:

C:\Users\elroi_svxj6s\source\repos\CoffeeNChill\CoffeeNChill

To run the application:

cd "C:\Users\elroi_svxj6s\source\repos\CoffeeNChill\CoffeeNChill"
func start

The Functions host should expose:

POST http://localhost:7071/api/menu
GET  http://localhost:7071/api/menu
Postman Testing

Postman is used to test the CoffeeNChill REST API.

The local base URL is:

http://localhost:7071

A Postman environment can use:

baseUrl = http://localhost:7071

This allows requests to use:

{{baseUrl}}

instead of repeatedly entering the full URL.

Create Menu Item Test
Request
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/menu
Tests
pm.test("Status code is 201", function () {
    pm.response.to.have.status(201);
});

pm.test("Response contains success message", function () {
    const jsonData = pm.response.json();
    pm.expect(jsonData.message).to.eql("Menu item created successfully.");
});

pm.test("Created item has correct ID", function () {
    const jsonData = pm.response.json();
    pm.expect(jsonData.item.id).to.eql("COF-100");
});

pm.test("Created item has correct name", function () {
    const jsonData = pm.response.json();
    pm.expect(jsonData.item.name).to.eql("Cappuccino");
});
Get All Menu Items Test
Request
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/menu
Tests
pm.test("Status code is 200", function () {
    pm.response.to.have.status(200);
});

pm.test("Response is an array", function () {
    const jsonData = pm.response.json();
    pm.expect(jsonData).to.be.an("array");
});

pm.test("Menu contains items", function () {
    const jsonData = pm.response.json();
    pm.expect(jsonData.length).to.be.above(0);
});

pm.test("Cappuccino exists in menu", function () {
    const jsonData = pm.response.json();
    const item = jsonData.find(x => x.id === "COF-100");
    pm.expect(item).to.not.be.undefined;
});
Git and GitHub

Git is used for version control.

The CoffeeNChill repository contains the project source code, documentation and supporting files.

The project uses branches so that each team member can work on their assigned functionality independently.

Team members should merge their work into the main branch using Pull Requests rather than directly pushing changes to main.